                        "operator": 0,
                        "versionId": -1093178486,
                        "changesPatcherIO": 0
                    },
                    "text": "param p_output 0.5 @min 0 @max 1",
                    "varname": "p_output"
                }
            },
            {
                "box": {
                    "id": "obj-43",
                    "linecount": 69,
                    "maxclass": "comment",
                    "numinlets": 1,
                    "numoutlets": 0,
                    "patching_rect": [ 30.0, 553.0, 577.0, 1014.0 ],
                    "text": "ToTape9 — Airwindows (Chris Johnson)\nPage: https://www.airwindows.com/totape9/\n\nWhat it is: the most intense Airwindows tape emulation. Rather than\nwows-and-flutters bolted onto saturation, it models the transport:\nhead bump (low-frequency \"carrying\" of the tape), continous tape\n\"shape\" (a dull-then-open HF character), real flutter (sample-level pitch\nmodulation with an adjustable depth and speed), and custom tape bias\n(under-bias darkens the tape, over-bias distorts it). This RNBO port uses\nthe original normalized 0..1 values.\n\nParameters:\n\n- p_input (Input) 0..1, default 0.5\n  Drive into the tape. Internally gain = (2p)^2, so 0.5 = unity, 0.0 =\n  roughly -12 dB... 1.0 = x4 (+12 dB). A steep quadratic taper: most of\n  the \"drive\" feel lives in the top half of the knob.\n\n- p_tilt (Tilt) 0..1, default 0.5\n  A single tilt EQ: internally dublyAmount = p*2 (up to +2 treble lift) and\n  outlyAmount = -(1-p)*2 (down to -1 bass cut, hard clamped). 0.5 is flat;\n  right of center tilts up (treble emphasis, \"thinner but brighter\"), left\n  of center tilts down toward a dark/duller tape. Roughly +/- 12 dB of\n  tilt at the extremes.\n\n- p_shape (Shape) 0..1, default 0.5\n  Sets the internal two-way iirEncFreq/iirDecFreq lowpass pair: it is where\n  the tape's \"dark\" onset starts. Low Shape = deeply dark tape; high Shape\n  = bright. Roughly a corner-frequency control from very dark to nearly\n  full-band.\n\n- p_flutter (Flutter) 0..1, default 0.5\n  Tape flutter (pitch wobble) depth. Internally flutDepth = p^6 (steep):\n  small settings are subtle wow, huge settings are broken-tape chaos\n  (capped at a 498-sample pitch delay, about 11 ms at 44.1 kHz - that is\n  like a broken car cassette). Musically, keep it under 0.3 for realism.\n\n- p_flutspd (FlutSpd) 0..1, default 0.5\n  Flutter rate. Internally 0.02 * p^3 per sample, a cubic taper: from a\n  slow-consuming tape winding (very slow, drifts like a real man) up to a\n  few Hz cyclic wow. Combined with Flutter depth you dial the \"bendiness\".\n\n- p_bias (Bias) 0..1, default 0.5\n  Real tape bias. 0.5 is calibrated; below the midpoint is UNDER-bias:\n  the sound gets darker, muffled, slightly compressed. Above the midpoint\n  is OVER-bias: brighter, more distortion, eventually aggressive and\n  \"dirty\". (Internally bias = 2p - 1, then split into underBias /\n  overBias terms.)\n\n- p_headbmp (HeadBmp) 0..1, default 0.5\n  Bass emphasis of the transport (head bump amount). At modest values it\n  adds warmth/body around the low mids in a way EQ can't (it interacts\n  with flutter phase), and at high values it can become a bumpy or\n  \"too round\" character - controlled by headfrq next.\n\n- p_headfrq (HeadFrq) 0..1, default 0.5\n  Tuning of the head bump region. Together with HeadBmp it moves the bump\n  along the low-mid / upper-bass spectrum so you can make it sit around\n  kick fundamentals, subbass, or intelligibility of bass guitar.\n\n- p_output (Output) 0..1, default 0.5\n  Output trim. Internally outputGain = p*2, so 0.5 = unity and 1.0 = x2\n  (+6 dB) - the module also outputs a slightly hotter signal by design\n  (a \"louder tape\" by design that clips a little at the edges).\n\nMusical notes: a\"butter\" 2-buss tape. Typical master settings: Tilt 0.55,\nShape 0.65, Flutter 0.1, FlutSpd 0.2, Bias as taste, HeadBmp 0.4-0.5 and\nOutput 0.5. Push Input for a \"hot master\" and then use Output to stop\npumping (as with all Airwindows tools, range is relative - listen).\n",
                    "varname": "paraminfo_comment"
                }
            }
        ],
